const lorePages=[['geography','neverland','The Island Made True','Neverland responds to children’s imagination rather than ordinary geography. The reading map fixes recurring sites for continuity while preserving the novel’s dreamlike distances.','island-sighted'],['fairy-rule','fairies','Fairy Life and Belief','Fairies are small enough to contain one dominant feeling at a time. Their survival is linked to children’s belief, made explicit when Tinker Bell drinks poison.','tink-home'],['flight-rule','fairies','How Flight Works','Peter’s example, happy thoughts, and fairy dust make human flight possible; fatigue and inattention remain dangerous once the journey begins.','learning-flight'],['forgetting','time','Neverland Forgetting','Time does not hold Peter as it holds Wendy. His memory drops people and promises, allowing adventures to repeat while other lives accumulate consequences.','peter-memory'],['editorial-calendar','time','Editorial Calendar','The novel leaves much of Neverland’s duration deliberately vague. Event dates use a transparent editorial chronology: consecutive London nights, several weeks on the island, one year to the first spring visit, and approximate twenty-year generational jumps.','darling-beginnings'],['sources','sources','Text, Maps, and Illustrations','Structure follows the 1911 public-domain Peter and Wendy text at Project Gutenberg. F. D. Bedford’s public-domain plates illustrate places and objects. Portraits and six navigable fantasy maps are original PlotWeave assets made specifically for this example; maps are interpretive rather than canonical.','darling-beginnings']].map(([s,cat,title,body,visible])=>({...base,id:id('lore',s),categoryId:id('lore-category',cat),title,body,tags:[],coverImageId:s==='sources'?I('bedford-i005'):null,linkedEntityIds:[],visibleFromEventId:EV(visible)}))
